# Fork modularity

- Keep fork features in focused domain modules; shared upstream files should contain small, explicit calls or references.
- Preserve upstream improvements and public settings contracts during merges. Do not copy whole upstream implementations merely to avoid integration work.
- Pipeline owns UI workflow and input sequencing. Go owns complex recognition and policy. Python research tools should not become hidden production dependencies.
- Keep combat extensions in dedicated autofight files; preserve flat JSON settings and native timeline ordering through contract tests.
- Keep custom schema conditions in tools/schema/extensions by domain, with separate action and recognition entry points. Shared schema registries retain one extension reference each.
- Keep inn navigation, kitchen scheduling, recognition, and run-state accounting in separate modules with explicit inputs and results.
- Prefer stable IDs, resource overlays, and narrow adapters over duplicated nodes or cross-domain conditionals.
- Refactor incrementally without changing behavior; verify the integration contract and affected domain before merging.
- Avoid speculative plugin frameworks. Add an abstraction only when it removes an existing coupling or duplication.
